374 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (There will be moments in early childhood settings where the risk develops so quickly that an educator does not have time to work through every calming strategy before stepping in. A child may be climbing over a fence, moving towards a road, lifting a heavy object towards another child, or becoming so physically unsafe that somebody is likely to be hurt if the adult waits.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
375 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (In those moments, keeping everyone safe becomes the immediate priority. ACECQA is clear that physical restraint should only be used in an emergency situation, so if physical intervention becomes necessary, we should use only the physical support needed to interrupt the immediate danger and stop as soon as that risk has passed.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
377 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Those few words help the child understand that we are responding to safety rather than trying to punish or overpower them.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Once everybody is safe, what happens next matters too. We can reduce our language, soften our body position, give the child time to recover and reconnect without immediately expecting an apology or explanation while their nervous system is still highly activated.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (Knowing the child well can also help us notice the smaller signs that appear before things become unsafe. If we know a child is likely to climb, bolt, throw or lash out when overwhelmed, we may be able to offer movement, more space, a quieter environment or a familiar adult earlier, before physical intervention becomes necessary.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (If significant physical intervention has been required, educators should also follow their service procedures for recording what occurred, informing the appropriate leader and communicating with families where required.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 195 0 R
/E (That is the part I do not want us to lose. Keeping everyone safe matters, but so does what we learn from the moment afterwards.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [7]
>>
